## What the connector does

The connector lets Claude work inside your StoryBook team. Claude writes the ideas, story, screenplay, shots and dialogue. StoryBook checks what Claude writes, stores it, and analyses how your episodes perform.

- **StoryBook makes no AI call of its own** for work Claude does. Nothing from a Claude session is sent to Gemini.
- **The web app is unchanged.** The Generate buttons still use Gemini exactly as before. You can use either one, or both, on the same episode.
- **Claude acts as you.** It sees only the team you pick, and only what you allow.

## Add the connector in Claude

1. In Claude, open **Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector**.
2. Paste the connector URL: `https://<your StoryBook domain>/api/mcp`. The same URL is shown in StoryBook under **Settings → Connected apps → Add StoryBook to Claude**.
3. Claude sends you to StoryBook. Sign in with your usual StoryBook account.
4. The page reads "Claude wants to connect to StoryBook". Pick the team Claude may work in.
5. Under **Allow it to**, tick what Claude may do (see the scopes below), then click **Allow**. **Deny** cancels the connection.


![The consent page: Claude wants to connect to StoryBook, with the team list and the three scopes, read, write and render, and the Deny and Allow buttons](/images/docs/claude-connector/consent-page.png)

A connection belongs to one team. To let Claude work in a second team, add the connector again and pick the other team.

### Without sign-in: a personal access token

Some tools cannot sign in through a browser. For those, create a token yourself.

1. Open **Settings → Connected apps** in your team.
2. Under **New personal access token**, give the token a name, such as the device that will use it.
3. Choose its scopes and click **Create token**.
4. Copy the token when it appears, then click **I have saved it**. **It is shown once.** StoryBook keeps only a fingerprint, so a lost token cannot be shown again. Create a new one instead.
5. Give the token to your tool as a bearer token in the `Authorization` header.

![Settings, Connected apps: after Create token, the token is shown once with Copy and I have saved it buttons](/images/docs/claude-connector/connected-apps-token.png)

A token works for one team and one person, and can be revoked at any time.


![The story page of an episode written by Claude, with the Claude via MCP badge under the stage tabs](/images/docs/claude-connector/studio-written-by-claude.png)

## What each scope allows

You choose the scopes when you approve the connection or create the token. Pick the least Claude needs.

| Scope | Shown as | Allows |
|---|---|---|
| `studio:read` | Read your studio | Read projects, episodes, stage content, assets and analytics. |
| `studio:write` | Write to your studio | Create and edit the inputs you would type yourself, generate stages, and add notes, tags and experiments. |
| `studio:render` | Start renders | Start ElevenLabs voice, music and sound-effect renders (`start_voice_render`, `start_audio_render`) and check on them (`get_render_progress`). **These cost money.** |

Publishing is not on the list. Publishing to a platform always stays a button in the web app.

## The workflow

Claude follows the same stage order as the web studio: **ideation, story, screenplay, shots, audio cues**. A stage opens when the one before it has content. Claude can read a short guide to this order from the connector itself, so you can simply ask it to "work on episode 3".

Each generated stage goes through the same four steps.

1. **Start.** Claude starts a generation for one stage of one episode. StoryBook answers with the brief: your project settings, the characters and locations, the earlier stages, the shape the answer must take, and a quality checklist.
2. **Write and submit.** Claude writes the content from the brief and submits it part by part. For shots, a part is one scene.
3. **Corrections.** If a part does not fit the required shape, StoryBook sends back the errors field by field. Claude fixes the part and submits it again. Nothing is saved to your episode yet.
4. **Finalize.** When every part is accepted, Claude finalizes. StoryBook saves the whole stage in one step, the same way it saves a Gemini result. Shots then continue into audio cues.

If Claude stops half way, nothing half-written reaches your episode.

### While Claude is working

The studio page for that stage shows a banner: **Claude is working on this**, followed by what Claude is writing, which app it came from and when it started. While the banner is up, the buttons that start a Gemini generation on that page are paused ("Generating here is paused until it finishes"), so the web app and Claude cannot overwrite each other. Click **Cancel** in the banner to end the run.

![The screenplay page with the Claude is working on this banner and its Cancel button](/images/docs/claude-connector/working-banner.png)


### Who wrote what

Each story, screenplay, shot and dialogue line carries an origin badge: **Gemini**, **Claude via MCP**, or **Edited** once you change it by hand. The story and screenplay show it as "Written by" under the stage tabs; each shot and dialogue line shows its own.

![The shot list: the first shot is badged Edited, the second Claude via MCP](/images/docs/claude-connector/origin-badges-shots.png)


### Going back

Every time a stage is written, StoryBook keeps the version it replaced. If you prefer what was there before, use **Restore previous version**, at the top right of that stage's page, then confirm with **Restore**. **Keep current** closes the dialog. What was there is kept too, so restoring again brings it back. Restore is paused while Claude is writing the stage.

![The Restore the previous version confirmation, with Keep current and Restore buttons](/images/docs/claude-connector/restore-confirm.png)

![The story page after restoring, with a Previous version restored message](/images/docs/claude-connector/restore-after.png)


### Team AI settings

Open **Settings → AI** in your team. Owners can turn **Allow server generation** (Gemini in the web app) and **Allow external generation (MCP)** (Claude over the connector) on or off, choose the **Default mode**, turn on **Use past episode performance in briefs** (off by default; ideation, story and shot briefs then include what worked in the project's earlier episodes, labelled with sample size), and click **Save AI settings**. At least one must stay on. Members can see the choice but not change it.

![Settings, AI: the two Allow toggles, the Default mode choice, the past episode performance toggle and the Save AI settings button](/images/docs/claude-connector/team-ai-settings.png)


## Revoke access

1. Open **Settings → Connected apps** in the team.
2. Under **Your connections**, find the connection. It shows its scopes, when it was last used, and whether it is active.
3. Click **Revoke**, then confirm.

![The revoke confirmation over the Your connections list](/images/docs/claude-connector/revoke-confirm.png)


Revoking takes effect on Claude's next call. Revoked connections stay in the list, marked **Revoked**, so you have a record.

![Your connections: a personal access token marked Revoked above an OAuth app marked Active with its Revoke button](/images/docs/claude-connector/your-connections.png)


**Changing your password revokes every connection you have**, in every team. Add the connector again afterwards.

## Limits

- **Rate limits.** Each connection may make 120 calls a minute, and 60 of those may be writes. Past that, Claude is told to wait and try again shortly.
- **Runs expire.** A generation that has been idle for 30 minutes expires. Nothing is saved from it, and the stage is free to start again. Any call Claude makes on the run keeps it alive.
- **No spend cap on Claude's work.** A team's daily Gemini spend cap (Team settings → AI) limits only Generate in StoryBook. Claude writing through the connector makes no model call from StoryBook and is never capped. ElevenLabs renders are not capped either, so watch `studio:render`.
- **One run per stage.** A second generation on the same stage of the same episode is refused until the first one finishes, expires or is cancelled.
- **Publishing stays in the web app.** Claude cannot publish an episode. It can read the results afterwards.
- **No video rendering.** StoryBook does not render video. Claude can read the video prompts for each shot to use in an outside video tool.
